+## Media
+
+Games play videos and compressed sound through Media Foundation, DirectShow,
+the WM reader and DMOs, which all end in two Wine modules with Unix sides:
+winegstreamer (the decoders, and the parser behind DirectShow's splitters,
+the WM reader and Media Foundation's sources, which upstream reads through
+its GStreamer byte stream handler) and winedmo (upstream's own FFmpeg
+demuxer, which takes Media Foundation's sources instead with
+`HKCU\Software\Wine\MediaFoundation` `DisableGstByteStreamHandler` set to
+1). Upstream's winegstreamer Unix side is GStreamer,
+which the console does not have; patch 0490 implements its Unix interface
+(`dlls/winegstreamer/unixlib.h`) on FFmpeg instead, and leaves the PE side,
+which every frontend shares, as it is.
+
+FFmpeg 8.1.3 is built by `tools/build_ffmpeg_ps5.sh` from a checkout at its
+pinned commit (`--ffmpeg-source`), as static libraries with the payload SDK:
+every demuxer, decoder, parser and bitstream filter, libswscale and
+libswresample, and nothing else (no encoders, muxers, protocols, filters,
+devices or programs, nothing found on the build machine). Only its LGPL
+parts: the build stops if its configuration turns on anything under the GPL
+or nonfree. `winedmo.prx` and `winegstreamer.prx` each link the whole of it,
+with `wine/ps5/pw_ffmpeg_libc.c`: `gmtime_r` and `localtime_r`, which the
+console's libc lacks; `arc4random_buf` and `mkstemp`, which resolve only
+from the WebKit process's libraries (a title would call 0); and a wrapped
+`pthread_create` that gives the decoders' worker threads 1 MiB of stack,
+since the console's default is far smaller than a desktop's.
+
+The host build configures `--enable-winegstreamer --without-gstreamer
+--without-ffmpeg`: winegstreamer's PE side is built on every PC, and the PC's
+own media libraries change nothing. A prefix made by `wineboot` registers
+it; one made before has no registrations and wineboot does not run in a
+title, so PC5 appends them to such a prefix's `system.reg` once.
+
+What the backend keeps of GStreamer's: pushed samples are held (their
+refcount) until decoded; `MF_E_NOTACCEPTING` past the input queue's length;
+`MF_E_TRANSFORM_STREAM_CHANGE` before a decoder's first frame, and on a new
+frame size or output format, when the decoder may change format; partial
+audio reads; times counted from zero for input without them; outputs laid
+out as the frontends ask (plane alignment, a 2D buffer's stride, the display
+aperture, bottom-up frames). The parser demuxes on a thread of its own,
+reading through the frontends' read thread as before; each enabled stream's
+packets are queued (64 MiB past which demuxing waits, unless a reader is
+waiting on another stream, up to 256 MiB), and `get_buffer()` decodes and
+converts on the caller's thread. Streams the frontends know compressed (MPEG
+audio, AAC, WMA, H.264, WMV and VC-1, MPEG-1, Cinepak, Indeo 5) are given as
+they are when the frontend asks for that (AVC as a byte stream); the others
+are decoded. Seeks are exact unless a key frame is asked for. FFmpeg's own
+threads have no Wine TEB, so its messages wait in a buffer until a Wine
+thread empties it into the `winegstreamer` channel; the demuxer thread
+writes nothing.
+
+Not part of it: muxing (the MP3 and MPEG-4 sink factories, the sink writer)
+and the H.264 encoder, which report themselves unsupported. Measured on the
+PC against Wine's own conformance tests (`mf:transform` 73283 tests,
+`mfplat:mfplat` 5.7 million, `quartz` splitters and decoders, `wmvcore`,
+`qasf`): no failures but an MP3 file's duration estimated 8 ms short of
+GStreamer's, an MPEG-1 test that gets two frames where one was expected, and
+the sink writer's tests, which stop at muxing; several tests marked as
+failing on Wine now pass. With winedmo taking Media Foundation's sources,
+`mfplat:mfplat` has three failures more (`MF_MT_ALL_SAMPLES_INDEPENDENT`
+missing from an MPEG-4 file's AAC and MP3 streams, and a media source still
+referenced after its shutdown and release), though more of its tests and
+`mfreadwrite`'s marked as failing on Wine pass; so the byte stream handler
+stays the default, as upstream has it.
+
